{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"3f928b818a5978b37bb74f0a1124b6d0249a6c2d6819ac666f2a19464034f6a5","digest":{"component_or_area":"LLM usage logging and telemetry","key_entities":["PydanticAIRunner.run()","usage_log.record()","usage_log.record_failure()","interpret_failure()","REBAR_USAGE_LOG","src/rebar/llm/runner.py","src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py","step_identity ContextVar","OUTCOME_OK","OUTCOME_FAILED"],"problem_keywords":["usage logging","failed calls","llm failures","budget tracking","spend underreporting"],"propositions":["when an LLM call raises an exception, control leaves run() before reaching record(), so no usage row is written for failed calls","failed call spend is captured in exc.diagnostic but never reaches the usage log file, causing summarize() to under-report actual spend silently","the per-step attribution cannot see failed calls, making it impossible to identify which expensive steps failed versus succeeded","a successful call must be distinguishable from a failed call in the usage log by an explicit outcome field, not inferred from a missing field","moving record() across the exception boundary risks double-counting if a call fails and is retried, requiring the failure and success paths to be mutually exclusive"]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787443195039223001,"uuid":"ac52dfe3-4c22-4a24-8c64-d998d50a7446"}